## Solution

Let $\alpha = 1+\sqrt{2}$ and $\beta = 1-\sqrt{2}$. Then
$$\alpha + \beta = 2, \qquad \alpha\beta = (1+\sqrt{2})(1-\sqrt{2}) = 1-2 = -1,$$
so $\alpha,\beta$ are the two roots of $x^2 - 2x - 1 = 0$.

**Integrality.** Define $S_n = \alpha^n + \beta^n$. Since $\alpha,\beta$ satisfy $x^2=2x+1$, the sequence satisfies
$$S_n = 2S_{n-1} + S_{n-2}, \qquad S_0 = 2,\; S_1 = 2.$$
By induction $S_n \in \mathbb{Z}$ for all $n \ge 0$.

**Size of $\beta^{3000}$.** We have $\beta = 1-\sqrt{2} \approx -0.41421356$, so $|\beta| = \sqrt{2}-1 \approx 0.41421356$. Since $3000$ is even,
$$\beta^{3000} = (\sqrt{2}-1)^{3000} > 0.$$
Estimate its magnitude:
$$\log_{10}|\beta| = \log_{10}(0.41421356) \approx -0.38278,$$
$$\log_{10}\beta^{3000} = 3000 \cdot (-0.38278) \approx -1148.3.$$
Hence
$$0 < \beta^{3000} \approx 10^{-1148.3} \ll 10^{-100}.$$

**Decimal expansion.** Since
$$\alpha^{3000} = S_{3000} - \beta^{3000} = (S_{3000}-1) + \bigl(1 - \beta^{3000}\bigr),$$
and $S_{3000}$ is a positive integer while $0 < \beta^{3000} < 10^{-1148}$, the fractional part of $\alpha^{3000}$ is
$$1 - \beta^{3000} = 0.\underbrace{999\ldots 999}_{\text{at least }1148\text{ nines}}\ldots$$
More precisely, since $\beta^{3000}\approx 5\times 10^{-1149}$, the first $1148$ digits after the decimal point are all $9$.

In particular, the $100$th digit after the decimal point is

$$\boxed{9}$$

